Over view of TCL 
Tkl is a interpreted high level language
interpretor---> means line by line.
c and c++ ---> compiler language ---> it excecute entire code with one go.

Overview of TCL: TCL stands for Tool Command Language. It is an open-source, high-level, and interpreted scripting language. It was created by John Ousterhout in 1988 and is now maintained by the TCL community. TCL is known for its simple syntax, making it easy to learn and use, and its ability to integrate with other languages and systems.

Simple Syntax: TCL's, L's syntax is very simple, making it easy to learn and use. It "It uses a combination of commands, variables, and control structures to perform tasks. It does not have a strict syntax like other languages, which makes it more flexible and easier to use.

Applications: TCL is used in a wide range of applications, including embedded systems, network management, and system administration. It is also used in the development of graphical user interfaces (GUIs) and automation of interactive programs. TCL can be used to automate repetitive tasks, create custom tools, and create scripts that can be run on a variety of platforms.

-------------
Variables and Data Types: TCL has a simple and flexible variable handling system. It supports different data types such as integers, floating-point numbers, strings, lists, and arrays. TCL also has a built-in type conversion function, which makes it easy to convert between different data types.

Control Structures: TCL has a variety of control structures, such as loops and conditionals, that can be used to control the flow of a program. TCL also supports the use of procedures, which can be used to group commands together and make a script more organized and readable.

Regular Expressions: TCL supports regular expressions, which are used to match patterns in strings. This is useful for tasks such as validation, data extraction, and text manipulation. TCL's regular expression support is based on the POSIX standard, which means it is compatible with regular expressions used in other languages and tools.

Object-Oriented Programming: TCL supports object-oriented programming. which allows for the creation of custom data types and the use of inheritance and encapsulation. This makes it easier to create complex and modular programs.

---------------
Built-in functions: TCL has a wide range of built-in functions that can be used for various tasks, such as file system operations, network I/O, and string manipulation. These functions make it easy to perform common tasks and can be used to create more advanced scripts.

Frameworks and Libraries: TCL can be extended using various libraries and frameworks, such as TK for GUI development, Expect for automating interactive programs, and TCLX for providing additional utility commands. These libraries and frameworks can be used to add functionality to TCL and make it more powerful.

Real-world examples: TCL is used in a wide range of real-world applications. For

example, in the field of network management, TCL scripts can be used to automate the configuration of network devices. TCL is also used in the field of embedded systems, where it can be used to create custom scripts for controlling and monitoring devices.




