example 3: 
calculate the area of the cell using special variables. passing coordinates and its values using special variables.

puts "argument list : $argv"
puts "count of the arguments: $argc"
lassign $argv a b c d e f g h
set area [expr ($d -$b)*($h - $f)]
puts "area of the cell is: $area"


example 4:

1.calculate the area of the cell using special variables. passing coordinates and its values using special variables.
2. if we pass the incorrect arguments:
print error
3. we need to check x2 > x1 and y2 > y1
print the error.
4. if we pass the coordinates in different order we have to cal area of the cell.
5. all is correct, do the area calculation.

solution: 
puts "argument list : $argv"
puts "count of the arguments: $argc"
lassign $argv a b c d e f g h


if {$argc != 8} {
puts "Error: Insuffient arguments: The required arguments is 8"
} elseif {$b > $d} {
puts "Error: x1 is greater than x2"
} elseif {$f > $h} {
puts "Error: y1 is greater than y2"
} else {

# a		b 		c		d		e		f		g	h
# x1	10		x2		40		y1		40		y2	60
# 0		1		2		3		4		5		6	7
for {set i 0} {$i <= $argc} {incr i 2} {
switch [lindex $argv $i] {
"x1" {set x1 [lindex $argv  $i+1]} 
"x2" {set x2 [lindex $argv  $i+1]}
"y1" {set y1 [lindex $argv  $i+1]}
"y2" {set y2 [lindex $argv  $i+1]} 
}
}

set area [expr ($x2 -$x1)*($y2 - $y1)]
puts "area of the cell is: $area"
}

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FILE HANDLING OPERATIONS:

File handling in tcl refers to the process of reading from and writing to files using tcl commands.
Tcl provides a set of commands to open, read, write , and close files. The basic step for file handling in tcl are as follows:

Open a file using the "open" command. This command takes two arguments: the name of the file to be opened mode and the mode in which the file should be opened (read,write, or append)

set read [open "new.txt" r]

Read from or write using the appropriate commands. the "gets" command is used to read a single line from a file, while the "read" command  be used to read a specific number of bytes. the "puts" command is used to write a string to a file.

Close the file using the "close" command when finished.

# how to read the single line of the file.
set file [open ""file_name" r]
set data [gets $file]
puts $data

# it reads the line by line of the file

set a [open "program1.tcl" r]
while {[gets $a line ] >= 0} {
puts $line
}
close $a


# read entire content of the file:
set file [open "program1.tcl" r]
set data [read $file]
puts $data




