}

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FILE HANDLING OPERATIONS:

File handling in tcl refers to the process of reading from and writing to files using tcl commands.
Tcl provides a set of commands to open, read, write , and close files. The basic step for file handling in tcl are as follows:

Open a file using the "open" command. This command takes two arguments: the name of the file to be opened mode and the mode in which the file should be opened (read,write, or append)

set read [open "new.txt" r]

Read from or write using the appropriate commands. the "gets" command is used to read a single line from a file, while the "read" command  be used to read a specific number of bytes. the "puts" command is used to write a string to a file.

Close the file using the "close" command when finished.

# how to read the single line of the file.
set file [open ""file_name" r]
set data [gets $file]
puts $data

# it reads the line by line of the file

set a [open "program1.tcl" r]
while {[gets $a line ] >= 0} {
puts $line
}
close $a


# read entire content of the file:
set file [open "program1.tcl" r]
set data [read $file]
puts $data


# read entire content of the file:
set file [open "program1.tcl" r]
set data [read $file]
puts $data

--
write : w

W: This mode opens th file for writing, but if the file is alredy exists, its content are truncated.(i.e, deleted). If the file is desent exit, a new file is created.

set w [open "new.txt" w]
puts $w "wlcome to vlsiguru"
close $w

---
Append: 
a: This mode opens the file for writing, but if the file already exists, new data is appended to the end of the file. If the file doesn't exist, a new file is created.


set ap [open "example.txt" a]
puts $ap "welcome to vlsiguru"
close $ap


r+----> This mode opens the file for reading and writing. starting at the begining of the file. 
If file is doesn't exist, an error will show.

set a [open "new.txt" r+]
puts $a "hello"
close $a



w+ ---> This mode opens the file for reading and writing, but if the file alredy exists, it contents are truncated. (i.e, deleted). If the file doesn't exist, a  new file created.

set a [open "new.txt" w+]
puts $a "hello"
close $a



a+ ---> This mode opens the file for reading and writing, but if the file already exists, new data is appended to the end of the file. If file is does't exists a new file is created.



set a [open "new.txt" a+]
puts $a "hello"
close $a

