1. Modular programming: procedure allows you to break up your code into smaller, more manageble pieces. This makes your code esiear to read, understand and maintain.

2. Code reusability: Once you define a procedure, you can call it multiple times from different parts of your code.

3. Parameterization: procedure can take input parameters, which aloows you to pass a different values. this makes your code more flexible and adaptable.

4. Encapsulation: procedures can be used to encapsulate complex logic or alogorithms, making it easier to reuse or share that logic with other developers.

ex:
proc add {a b} {
set c [expr $a + $b]
return $c
}
puts [add 10 10]



1. write a proc with variable number of arguments?

proc sum {args} {
set s 0
foreach arg $args {
incr s $arg    
# s+1
}
return $s
}

puts [sum 1 5 6 2]
puts [sum 1 5 ]
puts [sum 1 5 10 20 6 2]
puts [sum 1 5 6 2 5]

---
Arguments with default values/implicit arguments

Implict arguments in TCL procedure may have implicit values.
An implict arguments values is used if no explict values is provided.

proc sum  {a {b 2} } {
puts $a
puts $b
}
sum 10 20


proc sum  {a {b 2} } {
set c [expr $a + $b]
return $c
}
puts [sum 10 20]


# how to return multiple values in the proc.
# the return command passes one value to caller.
there is often a need to return the multiple values. in such cases we can make return list.


proc sum  {a b} {
set m1 [expr $a + $b]
set m2 [expr $a - $b]
set m3 [expr $a * $b]
set m4 [expr $a / $b]
return [list $m1 $m2 $m3 $m4]
}
puts [sum 20 10]




Scope of the variables in the proc:

local variables:
A variable declared inside a procedure has a procedure scope/ local verible.

proc sum  {} {
set a 10
puts $a
}
#puts $a
sum

global variables:

Any variable which is declared outside the proc. we can also use thi variable inside proc.

set b 20
proc sum  {a} {
global b
set c [expr $a + $b]
return $c
}
puts [sum 10]

set d [expr $b * $b]
puts $d

---
Recursion in the proc:
Recursion is way yo call the procedure itself repeatedly.

Recursion is a way of declaring proc in which the proc being defined is applies within its own defination.

1. adding of the sum by the given arguments:

proc maneesh {a} {
set sum 0
for {set i 1} {$i<=$a} {incr i} {
 set sum [expr ($sum + $i)]
}
puts $sum
}
maneesh 10

2. write for the factorial if we give argument value.

proc fac {a} {
if {$a >0} {
return [expr $a * [fac [expr $a-1]]] 
} else {
return 1
}
}
puts [fac 5]

--
In Tcl a "nested proc" is a procedure that is defined inside another procedure. This means that the nested procedure is only visible and accesible within the scope of the outer procedure. Once the outer procedure returns or is exited, the nested procedure is no longer accessible.

proc outer_proc {} {
	proc inner_proc {name} {
	puts "hello world"
	} 
	inner_proc "vlsiguru"
}
outer_proc 


or
proc outer_proc {a} {
	proc inner_proc {name} {
	puts "hello world"
	return $name
	} 
	puts [inner_proc "vlsiguru"]
return $a
}
puts [outer_proc tcl_hyd]  
